WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:15.180
On the 13th of April 1970, three astronauts&nbsp;
were broadcasting back to earth, beaming a rare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.380
live look inside a spaceship on one of&nbsp;
the most important missions in history.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:25.140
Jim Lovell, Jack Swigert, and Fred&nbsp;
Haise were heading for the moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:29.880
where, if all went to plan, Lovell and&nbsp;
Haise would become the fifth and sixth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:34.380
humans to walk on its surface.&nbsp;
All three were in high spirits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:38.760
chuckling and chortling for the camera -&nbsp;
even playing practical jokes on each other.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.880
Back on earth at Mission Control in Houston,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:47.400
Texas, the mood was equally buoyant. The&nbsp;
first 55 hours had gone so smoothly that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:53.880
Capsule Communicator Joe Kerwin carped that he&nbsp;
and the rest of the team were bored to tears.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:59.640
In hindsight, tempting fate with practical&nbsp;
jokes and talk of boredom was a bad idea.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:04.260
Because if there's one place you don't want to&nbsp;
dare the universe to make an example of you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.860
it's in a tiny spaceship 200,000 miles from home.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.440
Lovell closed the broadcast by wishing&nbsp;
the people of earth a pleasant evening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:26.340
sporting a beaming grin on his face.&nbsp;
However, it didn’t stay there for long.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.740
Because shortly after, Jim Lovell, Jack&nbsp;
Swigert, and Fred Haise were fighting for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:40.680
their lives in a crippled spaceship that seemed&nbsp;
certain to become their cold, cramped coffin.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:46.920
The question on their - and everyone else's -&nbsp;
lips was simple: would they make it home alive?

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:52.680
This is the remarkable story of the most&nbsp;
magnificent mishap in the history of space flight.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.020
This is Apollo 13.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.760
It's easy to assume that the eyes of&nbsp;
the world were on the crew of Apollo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.860
13 as they blasted off from the Kennedy&nbsp;
Space Centre in Florida. But the truth is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.440
most people didn't even know&nbsp;
the launch was happening.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:39.660
Apollo 13 was to be the fifth manned mission&nbsp;
to the moon, and its astronauts would be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:47.100
third crew to step foot on its surface - what&nbsp;
would be huge news today was *old* news in 1970.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.840
As a result, that broadcast I just&nbsp;
mentioned - the one that should have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:56.580
given the public an incredible insight&nbsp;
into life on a space shuttle heading to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:02.820
the moon - not a single station picked it&nbsp;
up, and it was never actually shown on TV.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:09.420
If it *had* been, those who watched would&nbsp;
have witnessed the stillness before space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:15.720
exploration's most severe storm. Because,&nbsp;
within 6 and a half minutes of the Apollo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:22.800
13 crew waving goodbye to an absent audience,&nbsp;
a large chunk of their spacecraft exploded.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:28.680
A loud bang bellowed and scores&nbsp;
of warning lights simultaneously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.920
blinked on. It was Jack Swigert&nbsp;
who first reported the bad news&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:36.240
to Mission Control in what is now one&nbsp;
of the most famous quotes in history:

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.640
“Houston, we have a problem…”

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:43.180
...Except, that isn't actually&nbsp;
what he said. Swigert's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:50.400
actual proclamation was the pronouncedly less&nbsp;
pithy - 'Okay, Houston, we've had a problem here.'

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.260
The misqoute was made famous by the&nbsp;
1995 film 'Apollo 13,' but it wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.260 --> 00:05:00.180
so much a mistake as a deliberate&nbsp;
rewriting of history. The modified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:04.980
line - particularly the change from past to&nbsp;
present tense - was deemed more dramatic.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:09.120
Anyway, the exact wording doesn't&nbsp;
matter. What mattered was that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:11.940
crew of Apollo 13 were now officially fucked.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:19.380
So many of the spaceship's systems went south&nbsp;
so swiftly that the guys in Mission Control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.640
assumed their instruments were playing&nbsp;
up. Jim Lovell had another theory - he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:28.500
thought they'd been hit by a meteor.&nbsp;
As it happens, they were both wrong - a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:33.420
faulty wire had caused an explosion that&nbsp;
destroyed the ship's vital oxygen tanks.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:40.920
Short of the insta-death of a decompression&nbsp;
incident, it was just about the worst possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:47.340
thing that could happen. It wasn't just that the&nbsp;
crew needed oxygen for, you know, not dying - the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:53.160
ship did, too. The Apollo 13 command module -&nbsp;
which housed the crew's accommodation and many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:59.100
of the ship's critical systems - was powered&nbsp;
by 3 electrochemical fuel cells, and the cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:04.920
themselves were fed by a combination of hydrogen&nbsp;
and oxygen. No oxygen meant no electricity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.320
and no electricity meant almost nothing&nbsp;
in the entire command module would work.

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:18.300
Before the explosion, the Apollo 13 crew had been&nbsp;
speeding towards the moon and a place in history.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:22.680
After it, they were scrambling simply to survive.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.680
The first and most pressing obstacle to overcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:29.700
was the minor concern that the&nbsp;
oxygen was about to run out.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:35.940
The Apollo 13 spacecraft was split into three&nbsp;
parts - the command module, the service module,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:41.340
and the lunar module. Without power the&nbsp;
command module was essentially dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:46.080
and the service module, which housed the&nbsp;
recently exploded oxygen tanks and other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:52.620
consumables - contained no living space. That&nbsp;
left the lunar module - the craft that would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:58.080
taken Lovell and Haise down to the surface of the&nbsp;
moon had the mission not gone to absolute shit.

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:02.820
The lunar lander was a tight fit&nbsp;
for three grown men and it hadn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.580
been designed for prolonged occupation - it didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.580 --> 00:07:10.140
even have seats and in places its outer&nbsp;
shell was less than a millimetre thick.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:15.240
That was the bad news. But the good news was&nbsp;
life saving - the lunar module was essentially a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:20.220
self-contained unit - it had its own life support&nbsp;
system and was powered by dedicated batteries.

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:25.080
With oxygen - and therefore power -&nbsp;
rapidly depleting in the command module,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:29.400
the astronauts made a mad dash for the&nbsp;
lunar module. Swigert stayed behind to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:34.620
shut down the sundry electrical systems to&nbsp;
save what little power was left (they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:39.240
going to need it if they ever made it back to&nbsp;
earth), while Lovell got to work transferring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:44.880
vital navigational data from the computer in the&nbsp;
command module to the one in the lunar module.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:50.640
Unfortunately for Lovell, the computers&nbsp;
used different coordinate systems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:56.880
so he was forced to do some on-the-fly algebra&nbsp;
to convert between the two. Considering any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:01.440
errors would likely have meant death&nbsp;
for him and his friends, it was a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:07.140
like doing an undergraduate mathematics exam&nbsp;
while the universe held a gun to his temple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:12.660
Luckily, Lovell was an absolute badass&nbsp;
and he passed with flying colours.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.900
For the next three and a half days, the lunar&nbsp;
module would become the astronaut's lifeboat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:24.960
in the sea of space - a tiny oasis in&nbsp;
the deadliest desert in the universe.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:29.880
But while the immediate&nbsp;
'oh-shit-we're-all-about-to-die' danger had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:35.220
passed, there was still the small matter of how&nbsp;
the hell they were going to get home - remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:39.180
at this point in the mission they were still&nbsp;
speeding away from Earth towards the moon.

00:08:39.180 --> 00:08:43.200
The most obvious course of action was&nbsp;
to fire the main thruster to turn the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:48.540
ship around. It was the fastest way to get&nbsp;
home, but it was also risky. It would mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:54.480
draining most of the remaining power from the&nbsp;
command module's fuel cells, and even worse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.480 --> 00:09:00.360
the thruster was located on the recently&nbsp;
exploded service module. Neither the crew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:06.060
nor mission control had any way of knowing&nbsp;
whether it'd been damaged in the blast.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:12.660
In a worst case scenario, firing it could have&nbsp;
blown the entire ship into a million pieces.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:20.880
That was far from ideal, so NASA's best and&nbsp;
brightest came up with a radical plan B.

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:28.320
You see, the service module wasn't the only part&nbsp;
of the ship equipped with a thruster - the lunar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:32.700
module had one too, designed to control the&nbsp;
lander’s descent down to the surface of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:37.140
moon. It was significantly less powerful&nbsp;
than the main thruster so an immediate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:43.140
u-ie was impossible. But it did have enough&nbsp;
power to nudge the ship's course just enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:49.560
to put it on an earth-reentry trajectory after&nbsp;
slingshotting around the moon. It would be much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.560 --> 00:09:55.080
slower than an immediate abort using the main&nbsp;
thruster, but it was also significantly safer.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:10:00.840
The decision ultimately lay with Flight&nbsp;
Director Gene Kranz, and after careful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:06.960
consideration he placed his bet on the&nbsp;
tortoise rather than the hare. As Apollo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:12.780
13 swung around the far side of the moon,&nbsp;
the crew performed a 34 second burn of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:18.000
lunar module's descent propulsion system to&nbsp;
put the craft back on a reentry trajectory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.920
followed by a second, longer burn to speed&nbsp;
up the journey home by around 12 hours.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:28.200
That last part was crucial, because&nbsp;
time was most certainly of the essence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.620
Plan B may have avoided the possible&nbsp;
game over screen of firing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:39.120
potentially-damaged main thruster. But&nbsp;
it came with grave dangers of its own.

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:44.280
The lunar module was designed to accommodate two&nbsp;
astronauts on the surface of the moon for about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:50.460
2 days. But after its entirely unexpected career&nbsp;
change from lunar lander to long term lifeboat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:54.960
it was going to have to support *3*&nbsp;
astronauts for 3 and a half days.

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:58.800
You don’t have to be pythagoras to&nbsp;
see that those sums didn't add up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:05.460
and every single consumable resource - energy,&nbsp;
food, water, and oxygen - would have to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:11.991
stretched to the absolute limit if the crew were&nbsp;
to stand any chance of returning to terra-firma.

00:11:11.991 --> 00:11:12.000
## Energy

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:16.800
The lunar module had its own batteries, but&nbsp;
they'd never been intended for such prolonged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:23.280
use. If they died, so too would the crew. So&nbsp;
all non-essential systems were shut down,,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:27.480
reducing energy consumption to&nbsp;
about one fifth of its typical rate.

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:32.700
Problem was, the ship's electrical systems&nbsp;
also happened to be an important source of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:37.080
passive heat. With most of them powered&nbsp;
down the temperature inside the ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.080 --> 00:11:41.760
plummeted to just 3 degrees Celsius&nbsp;
- about what you'd find in a fridge.

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:43.140
## Food and Water

00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:48.120
For the most part food wasn't an issue. The crew&nbsp;
still had access to the crippled Command Module,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:54.120
where there was plenty of grub floating around&nbsp;
- literally - although some of it froze.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:59.040
Water was a different story. There&nbsp;
was a small portable reserve aboard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.940
but most of it was supposed to be&nbsp;
made along the way as a byproduct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:07.680
of reactions in the ship's fuel&nbsp;
cells - you know, the broken ones.

00:12:08.700 --> 00:12:13.200
Many of the ship's critical systems&nbsp;
relied on water-cooling. So,in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:17.040
effort to make sure there was enough of&nbsp;
the wet stuff for both man and machine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:22.500
the astronauts were put on a water&nbsp;
ration of just 200 ml per person per day.

00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:24.000
## Oxygen

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:29.040
Surprisingly, oxygen wasn’t actually a concern.&nbsp;
Despite being designed for a two-day stay on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:33.840
the moon, the Lunar Module had contingency&nbsp;
built in, which included a large quantity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:39.840
of excess oxygen. Unfortunately, the crew also&nbsp;
had an excess of something much less desirable.

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:45.120
About a day and a half after relocating into&nbsp;
the lunar module, yet another warning light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.120 --> 00:12:49.380
started blinking: the carbon dioxide&nbsp;
level was getting dangerously high.

00:12:49.380 --> 00:12:53.640
The lunar module was equipped&nbsp;
with a CO2 filtration system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:58.500
but like everything else it hadn't been&nbsp;
designed for such prolonged and intensive use.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:04.380
The system relied on canisters of&nbsp;
CO2-scrubbing lithium hydroxide pellets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:07.740
but they could only absorb so&nbsp;
much before becoming saturated.

00:13:08.580 --> 00:13:13.680
Luckily there were plenty of spare canisters&nbsp;
in the command module - un-fucking-luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:19.680
they were square shaped, and incompatible with&nbsp;
the round filter housing in the lunar module.

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:25.680
In hindsight, that was a huge oversight - there&nbsp;
was no reason whatsoever for the canisters to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:31.500
be incompatible across the two modules. But&nbsp;
it was far too late for finger pointing, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.500 --> 00:13:37.560
they didn't find a solution fast Lovell, Swigert,&nbsp;
and Haise were dead men walking… well, floating.

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:43.260
It was a life or death situation, but it was&nbsp;
also kind of hard not to appreciate the irony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:49.140
of NASA being forced to focus the combined&nbsp;
brainpower of its most brilliant engineers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:54.600
on the age-old problem of trying to&nbsp;
fit a square peg into a round hole.

00:13:54.600 --> 00:14:00.180
The solution they came up with was suitably&nbsp;
ingenious. Limited only by what would be available&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.180 --> 00:14:06.000
to the astronauts, they hashed out a design&nbsp;
for a filter adapter using a space suit hose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:12.420
several plastic sample bags, cardboard torn&nbsp;
from logbook covers, and a rolled up sock.

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:17.820
The ridiculous contraption was held&nbsp;
together with duct tape and looked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.820 --> 00:14:20.640
about as sophisticated as a crackhead in drag,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:26.940
but as soon as the astronauts finished putting&nbsp;
it together the CO2 level began to drop.

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:34.980
Despite having once again successfully managed&nbsp;
to not die, things were going from bad to worse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:40.380
The crew were forbidden from venting urine&nbsp;
out into space for fear the tiny jets of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:45.900
wee would propel the craft off course. And&nbsp;
after several days peeing in plastic bags,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.900 --> 00:14:47.880
there were pouches of piss all over the place.

00:14:47.880 --> 00:14:52.980
In summary, Lovell, Swigert, and&nbsp;
Haise were cold, they were dehydrated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:58.020
and they were perpetuallyassaulted by&nbsp;
plastic pockets of their own piss. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.020 --> 00:15:00.600
if Lovell and his crew thought&nbsp;
their luck was about to change,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:06.720
they were mistaken. Because as earth grew&nbsp;
ever larger outside the spacecraft’s windows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:13.440
Mission Control got in touch with yet more bad&nbsp;
news - they'd drifted off course. Instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:18.180
penetrating earth's atmosphere as intended, they&nbsp;
were going to bounce off it like a skimming stone.

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:23.040
If that happened there would be no&nbsp;
second chances - the spaceship would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:28.320
become their tomb and their frozen corpses&nbsp;
would remain in a loose orbit around earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.320 --> 00:15:31.200
for thousands - perhaps millions - of years.

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:36.540
Now, I know what you're thinking - they'd already&nbsp;
corrected their course once, why not just do it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:41.880
again? Unfortunately, it wasn't that easy. The&nbsp;
previous course correction had levraged the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:47.460
moon’s gravity in tandem with the thrusters.&nbsp;
This time - so close to earth and without the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.460 --> 00:15:52.980
moon's gravity to help out - they were going&nbsp;
to have to take matters into their own hands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.520 --> 00:15:58.980
literally. They powered down the guidance&nbsp;
computer (aka sat nav) to the ship’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:04.380
location was basically unknown, then,&nbsp;
they grabbed hold of the controls and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.380 --> 00:16:09.780
using the sun as a reference point,&nbsp;
manually lined the ship up for reentry.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:16.140
This was entirely unchartered territory - no&nbsp;
crew had ever attempted anything like it before.

00:16:16.140 --> 00:16:22.200
Get it wrong, and they would be&nbsp;
dead. Get it right and... well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:25.500
they still might be dead. But&nbsp;
at least they'd have a chance.

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:30.360
5 hours before scheduled reentry into&nbsp;
earth's atmosphere, Mission Control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:37.260
gave them the go ahead to execute the burn.&nbsp;
Under unimaginable pressure, Lovell, Swigert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:43.920
and Haise pulled off the unprecedented manoeuvre&nbsp;
perfectly. So perfectly, in fact, that by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:49.800
time they shut off the thrusters, they were within&nbsp;
half a degree of their intended angle of approach.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:52.560
Apollo 13 was coming home.

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:57.900
The lunar module had served the astronauts&nbsp;
remarkably well over the past three and a half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.900 --> 00:17:03.960
days, but the time had come to leave it behind.&nbsp;
Because only one part of the Apollo spacecraft&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:09.540
was capable of withstanding the enormous&nbsp;
temperatures of reentry - the command module.

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:14.940
There was of course one slight problem with that&nbsp;
- the crippled command module had been powered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.940 --> 00:17:20.400
down three and a half days earlier. Engineers on&nbsp;
the ground had spent the intervening time trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:24.720
to figure out how the hell they were going&nbsp;
to bring it back online again using only the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:30.300
lunar module's meagre battery. Nobody had ever&nbsp;
powered down a command module in space before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:36.660
and the team had to write an entirely new&nbsp;
procedure from scratch. It ran to over 500&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:42.300
individual steps, and it was down to the&nbsp;
absolutely bloody knackered, dangerously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.300 --> 00:17:49.560
dehydrated Jack Swigert to implement it in the&nbsp;
freezing cold command module with only torchlight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:55.200
to see by. Just in case that wasn't miserable&nbsp;
enough, any mistake meant almost certain death.

00:17:55.800 --> 00:18:01.200
To the immense relief of everyone concerned,&nbsp;
Swigert swaggered his way through the makeshift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.200 --> 00:18:07.740
process and the wounded Command Module slowly&nbsp;
flickered back to life. With that out of the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:14.400
the service and lunar modules were jettisoned off&nbsp;
into space, and Apollo 13 was ready for reentry.

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:22.620
Even then, having come so far and gone through&nbsp;
so much, nobody knew for sure if they’d make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:27.900
down to earth in one piece. The key concern was&nbsp;
the heat shield - it had been perilously close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:32.580
to the site of the oxygen-tank explosion,&nbsp;
and there was no way of knowing if it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.580 --> 00:18:37.980
been damaged in the blast. If the heat shield&nbsp;
failed, the astronauts would die a fiery death.

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:43.080
That was surely on their minds as they strapped&nbsp;
themselves in and prepared for the lights to go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:48.960
up on the final act of the most extraordinary&nbsp;
space opera in history. Would they see their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:55.020
families again, or were the seconds from an&nbsp;
agonising death? They were about to find out.

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:05.160
Back on earth, Apollo 13 had gone from&nbsp;
a mission nobody really cared about to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.160 --> 00:19:10.440
the most closely followed in history after&nbsp;
the Apollo 11 moon landing. There had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:15.960
round the clock news coverage of the astronauts'&nbsp;
plight, and an estimated 70 million people were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:21.540
watching events unfold on TV. Many millions&nbsp;
more had their eyes trained on the sky.

00:19:23.160 --> 00:19:26.940
Just to ratchet up the tension that&nbsp;
little bit further, there would be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.940 --> 00:19:31.980
4 minute radio blackout during reentry&nbsp;
as the immense heat interrupted comms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:42.060
The world held its breath as the seconds ticked&nbsp;
down, and when those 4 minutes were finally up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.060 --> 00:19:49.080
Houston tentatively radioed the command&nbsp;
module… and got no response whatsoever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:54.000
A full minute and a half passed&nbsp;
and still there was nothing.

00:19:54.000 --> 00:19:59.220
But just when it seemed certain the Apollo&nbsp;
astronauts perished at the very last hurdle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:59.220 --> 00:20:06.360
Jack Swigert's voice crackled into life&nbsp;
on the radio. Apollo 13 had made it.

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:11.100
The command module splashed down in the&nbsp;
South Pacific soon afterwards not far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:15.360
from American Samoa, where the crew&nbsp;
were picked up by the USS Iwo Jima.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:21.840
They were cold, they were dehydrated, they were&nbsp;
tired, and they were very, very glad to be home.

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:27.420
Jim Lovell, Jack Swigert, and Fred Haise may&nbsp;
not have walked on the surface of the moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.420 --> 00:20:31.740
but to this day they hold the record for&nbsp;
reaching the greatest distance from Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:37.980
in history. To put that another way, no human&nbsp;
beings before or since have ventured further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:44.400
away from our home planet than the Apollo 13&nbsp;
astronauts. Even more importantly, those three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:51.300
men played the starring roles in one of mankind's&nbsp;
greatest triumphs of cooperation and survival.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:54.240
Thanks for watching.

